 ## Background
-The optimization of the 3D Gaussian Splatting rasterization pipeline emerged alongside the initial publication of 3DGS [Parent](2812-3d-gaussian-splatting.md) [G]. As 3DGS gained popularity for novel view synthesis and SLAM, the computational cost of rendering millions of Gaussians became a primary bottleneck [G]. Early implementations used coarse bounding boxes for culling, leading to significant overdraw [G]. Subsequent works like Speedy-Splat [Child](3543-speedy-splat-rendering-pipeline.md) and AdR-Gaussian [Child](8365-adr-gaussian.md) introduced precise geometric culling and load balancing. Concurrently, hardware-oriented approaches [Child](7329-hardware-accelerated-gaussian-rasterization.md) explored dedicated silicon units for projection and sorting. The technique is widely used in real-time rendering applications, SLAM systems (e.g., SplaTAM variants), and neural rendering pipelines where frame rate is critical [G].
